import{_ as t,o as d,c as r,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BS0bAV7E.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Known issues","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"root/KNOWN_ISSUES.md","filePath":"root/KNOWN_ISSUES.md"}'),a={name:"root/KNOWN_ISSUES.md"};function o(i,e,n,l,h,c){return d(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="known-issues" tabindex="-1">Known issues <a class="header-anchor" href="#known-issues" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Known issues&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The running record of known defects and their status. The 2.0.0 release shipped with the seventeen issues below (found by the 2.0.0 postcheck, <code>audit/2.0.0-postcheck/</code> in the private mirror); <strong>all seventeen are fixed in 2.1.0</strong>. The v1-era issue archive lives in <code>docs/archive/KNOWN_ISSUES.md</code>.</p><h2 id="_2-0-0-issues-all-fixed-in-2-1-0" tabindex="-1">2.0.0 issues - all fixed in 2.1.0 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-0-0-issues-all-fixed-in-2-1-0" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.0.0 issues - all fixed in 2.1.0&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Class</th><th>Issue</th><th>Fixed in 2.1.0 by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-01</td><td>P0 CI/release gate</td><td>The v2.0.0 release code failed to COMPILE on the CI runners (tui_core.c in/out with an imm8 &quot;Nd&quot; constraint on port 0x3F8/0x3FD - ports &gt;= 0x100 must go through DX). All 15 v2.0.0 CI runs were red; the release shipped local-green/CI-red, undeclared.</td><td>Port I/O forced through <code>%dx</code> (&quot;d&quot; constraint, u16 operand); plus two further CI-environment traps found and fixed on the way: the runner GRUB (2.12+) dropped <code>efi_uga.mod</code> (module lists are now filtered against the installed GRUB), and gcc &gt;= 15 defaults to C23 where <code>typedef u8 bool</code> collides with the native <code>_Bool</code> keyword (core/types.h keeps the compiler&#39;s bool under C23, u8 under gnu11). CI is green again and is a hard release gate.</td></tr><tr><td>P-02</td><td>P1 release data</td><td>Release-note numbers contradicted each other (&quot;134 of the 251 commands moved&quot; vs &quot;251 = 99+133+19&quot;; 99+134+19=252).</td><td>The 2.1.0 release note uses the self-consistent ledger: 251 = 99 L0 + 133 SVC + 19 built-ins; v1 baseline 233, all mapped (INTERFACES.md Part 1c).</td></tr><tr><td>P-03</td><td>P1 compatibility contract</td><td>The v1-&gt;v2 mapping table had 231 rows for a 233-command baseline: <code>health export</code> and <code>health watch</code> were silently dropped by a no-space parse regex.</td><td>Generator fixed; Part 1c maps all 233, zero MISSING.</td></tr><tr><td>P-04</td><td>P1 interface truth</td><td>No per-entry 162-&gt;158 comparison; &quot;register-class interfaces gone&quot; was an over-general summary.</td><td>INTERFACES.md Part 2c: the per-entry table over the complete v1 Part 2 truth (278 declarations: 267 public, 6 internalized, 5 gone); interfaces.json reports the current 17/158 surface + the v1 fate fields.</td></tr><tr><td>P-05</td><td>P1 deletions</td><td>No deletion register; the two real deletions were never declared.</td><td>INTERFACES.md Part 2d declares all five deletions (init/probe plumbing) with their verified v2 replacements; the register-class statement is exact.</td></tr><tr><td>P-06</td><td>P1 docs</td><td>ARCHITECTURE.md described the v1 tree (kernel/, userprogs/, 233, 53 syscalls, 162, &quot;boots into oc&gt; by default&quot;).</td><td>Rewritten for the real tree (two rings, 54 syscalls, 17/158, ush default entry), EN + zh mirrors.</td></tr><tr><td>P-07</td><td>P1 process docs</td><td>versioning.md (no +21 offset), process.md (WP- tags, v1 gates), dev/build.md, dev/e2e-suites.md, manual/index.md, BUILDING.md L141 all v1-era.</td><td>All updated to the v2 reality (v-tags, six-suite matrix + L1 per-app gate, +21 offset documented, current numbers).</td></tr><tr><td>P-08</td><td>P1 test coverage</td><td>No per-app L1 testing; the v1 28-check user-program regression battery was absent from the 2.0.0 matrix.</td><td>The 2.1.0 matrix adds the L1 per-app record (ush + every embedded program executed); see docs/dev/e2e-suites.md.</td></tr><tr><td>P-09</td><td>P1 comments</td><td>The Makefile comment claimed tui/chinese were &quot;rewritten as L1 ring-3 apps&quot; while both trees are still compiled INTO the kernel.</td><td>Comment corrected (tui/chinese stay L0-resident; l1apps/ holds ush + the embedded programs only).</td></tr><tr><td>P-10</td><td>P2 website data</td><td>site-data.js stats.lines=130,011 vs the real 130,495; update_website.sh copied the stale stats block forward.</td><td>update_website.sh REGENERATES the stats block from docs/data/*.json; site numbers always match the data files.</td></tr><tr><td>P-11</td><td>P2 assets</td><td>The public repo main tree did not hold the release ISO/SRC (only the private mirror + gh-pages did), contradicting the release note.</td><td>2.1.0 assets are committed to the public main (website/public/downloads/) byte-identical with the Release/gh-pages.</td></tr><tr><td>P-12</td><td>P2 assets</td><td>website/public/update*.json still carried WP-13/build-259 manifests (top-level pair was 2.0.0/302).</td><td>update_website.sh writes BOTH copies byte-identical (top level + public/).</td></tr><tr><td>P-13</td><td>P2 evidence</td><td>The five suite logs the 2.0.0 evidence README referenced did not exist in either repo; the SRC file name in asset_hashes.txt was wrong.</td><td>The five logs are real files regenerated by the 2.1.0 run (evidence/), and asset_hashes.txt names the actual published files.</td></tr><tr><td>P-14</td><td>P2 counting caliber</td><td>commands.json count_l1_svc=133 vs the 142 real ush SVC table rows - 9 dual-homed names silently attributed to L0; interfaces.json still carried the v1 162 official count.</td><td>Caliber stated exactly in commands.json + INTERFACES.md Part 1b (142 rows, 9 dual-homed, ledger 251 unchanged); interfaces.json reports 17/158.</td></tr><tr><td>P-15</td><td>P2 docs</td><td>docs/manual/commands was a v1 artifact (234 pages, &quot;Registered in: shell/...&quot; paths); the 23 v2-new commands had no pages.</td><td>Fully regenerated: 251 pages (EN + zh) with real v2 homes, ghost-page purge, help hints from l1svc_defs.h.</td></tr><tr><td>P-16</td><td>P2 governance</td><td>The private mirror&#39;s SYNC-RULES.md described a tree layout that no longer exists; the 2.0.0 work log was not in-repo.</td><td>SYNC-RULES.md rewritten for the current layout; WORK_LOG.md extended through 2.1.0 (both in the private mirror).</td></tr><tr><td>P-17</td><td>P3 text defects</td><td>A docs/dev/index.md link flagged as &quot;ost-tests (missing h)&quot;; the wp14_site_v2 evidence dir name; the 379-file caliber note.</td><td>The link defect turned out to be a FALSE POSITIVE (byte-level inspection shows <code>[host-tests]</code> was always correct - a display artifact had eaten the <code>[h</code> sequence in the postcheck evidence; recorded honestly). The evidence dir naming is corrected for 2.1.0 and the file-count caliber is documented in the data note.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="open-issues" tabindex="-1">Open issues <a class="header-anchor" href="#open-issues" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Open issues&quot;">​</a></h2><p>One known issue is open at 2.1.0, found by the new L1 per-app gate itself and declared rather than hot-fixed (a scheduler/exception return-path change hours before release would be reckless; it is NOT one of the 17 and does not affect any standard suite):</p><ul><li><strong>Flaky wait4-vs-fork timing race (rare).</strong> Under same-boot test marathons (and once in ~35 fresh-boot rows), a wait4-sensitive user program (g6_exec_a) occasionally observed its wait4 return without a status write and then read an uninitialized stack word (garbage &quot;status = 142&quot; where the deterministic result is 138 = 128+SIGUSR1), with the child&#39;s output arriving after the parent&#39;s verdict line - i.e. the parent&#39;s wait4 returned before the child was (fully) visible to it. Isolated replays of the exact same protocol boot clean (138 PASS, correct serial order) every time. The kernel&#39;s wait4 is POSIX-correct on paper (it returns -1 without writing status when it sees no child); the race is in the fork/child-visibility window under interrupt timing. Recorded here and in the 2.1.0 delivery report; the kernel fix belongs to the next batch. Evidence: the per-app marathon logs quote &quot;child status = 142&quot; + the impossible print order; the fresh-boot record in evidence/2_1_0_release/ shows the deterministic 138 PASS run.</li></ul><p>Everything else known at 2.1.0 is in the fixed table above.</p>',8)])])}const m=t(a,[["render",o]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
